From 0000000000000000000000000000000000000000 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
Date: Wed, 30 Sep 2026 21:00:00 -0400
Subject: [PATCH] Compile for free-threaded Python 3.14

Compiling for a free-threaded 3.14 did not build, and once it did, compiled
code crashed, hung, leaked or computed wrong results as soon as threads ran
it at the same time. Everything the GIL used to make private to one thread
is now either made per call or done the way CPython 3.14 does it without
the GIL. Builds with the GIL are unchanged but for fixes that apply to them
as well.

Build: GC allocations are counted per thread without generations; delayed
frees ask the interpreter whether the world is stopped; immortal dictionary
keys have a negative count; the frame code reference uses the free-threaded
stack reference constructor; the GIL check reads the build configuration,
not the flags of the process running Nuitka; debug builds no longer force
inlining through function pointers or warn about helpers they do not use.

Memory: objects are allocated from the mimalloc heap for their kind, as
"_PyObject_MallocWithType" does, so the collector sees them and freed blocks
keep clear of reference counts lock-free readers touch. The delayed-free
queue shared with CPython releases the objects CPython queues instead of
freeing their memory, takes each item off before releasing it, links new
chunks and survives running out of memory. Free-threaded dictionary lookups
no longer leak a reference each; frames made per call are released. New
objects are initialized as CPython's "new_reference" does, and zeroed
without their header, which lock-free readers of reused memory may read.

Shared state: cached frames, static free lists and the module variable
cache are not used. Module variables and closure cells are read with a
reference of our own and written through a lock, as CPython's globals and
"PyCell_GetRef"/"PyCell_SetTakeRef" are; type attribute lookups take a
reference ("_PyType_LookupRef"); in-place operations reuse an object only
if "_PyObject_IsUniquelyReferenced"; list and dictionary operations,
concatenation and comparison use CPython's, which lock; counters are atomic
and code objects are completed once, under their lock. Constants are
made immortal as "_Py_SetImmortal" does, so threads sharing them never
count references to them, and "sum" takes list items with
"PyList_GetItemRef". References to builtins in generated code, and the
values of compiled cells being compared or printed, are taken with a
reference of our own, and the extension module loader walks "sys.modules"
under its lock.

Also fixed, with or without the GIL: "list.remove"/"list.count" with a
tuple argument, a double release in "list.index" with a bad stop, and a
leaked cell in "__reduce__".

Signed-off-by: Kevin Turcios <turcioskevinr@gmail.com>
